WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.434 line:10%
Here's a puzzle: a patient's muscles are weak —

2
00:00:03.434 --> 00:00:06.645 line:10%
yet the more they use them, the stronger they get.

3
00:00:06.645 --> 00:00:10.249 line:10%
Why? The answer is hiding at the neuromuscular junction.

4
00:00:10.249 --> 00:00:12.935 line:10%
This is Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome.

5
00:00:13.425 --> 00:00:18.185 line:10%
At the neuromuscular junction, a nerve impulse opens voltage-gated calcium channels.

6
00:00:18.185 --> 00:00:22.714 line:10%
Calcium floods in, driving synaptic vesicles to fuse and release acetylcholine —

7
00:00:22.714 --> 00:00:26.430 line:10%
which activates receptors on the muscle and triggers contraction.

8
00:00:26.921 --> 00:00:33.080 line:10%
In Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome, antibodies attack these presynaptic P/Q-type calcium channels.

9
00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:37.416 line:10%
With the channels blocked, far less calcium enters the nerve terminal.

10
00:00:37.906 --> 00:00:40.174 line:10%
Less calcium means fewer vesicles fuse —

11
00:00:40.174 --> 00:00:44.538 line:10%
so only a trickle of acetylcholine is released, and the muscle signal is weak.

12
00:00:44.538 --> 00:00:48.846 line:10%
Tellingly, strength briefly improves with repeated use, as calcium builds up.

13
00:00:49.384 --> 00:00:52.810 line:10%
This is where Firdapse comes in — amifampridine, or 3,4-DAP.

14
00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:57.338 line:10%
It blocks presynaptic potassium channels, prolonging the nerve's depolarization.

15
00:00:57.338 --> 00:00:59.428 line:10%
That holds the calcium channels open longer,

16
00:00:59.428 --> 00:01:03.028 line:10%
so more calcium slips through the channels that remain.

17
00:01:03.028 --> 00:01:05.814 line:10%
More calcium, more vesicles, more acetylcholine —

18
00:01:05.814 --> 00:01:07.382 line:10%
and the signal is restored.

19
00:01:07.872 --> 00:01:09.638 line:10%
It can't remove the antibodies —

20
00:01:09.638 --> 00:01:12.177 line:10%
so it simply squeezes more calcium in instead.

21
00:01:12.757 --> 00:01:13.598 line:10%
So, to recap.

22
00:01:13.598 --> 00:01:18.639 line:10%
LEMS is driven by autoantibodies against presynaptic P/Q-type calcium channels.

23
00:01:18.639 --> 00:01:22.065 line:10%
Less calcium influx means less acetylcholine release —

24
00:01:22.065 --> 00:01:23.810 line:10%
and weak muscle activation.

25
00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:27.753 line:10%
Unlike myasthenia gravis, strength improves with repeated use.

26
00:01:27.753 --> 00:01:32.213 line:10%
It's often paraneoplastic, classically tied to small-cell lung cancer.

27
00:01:32.213 --> 00:01:34.864 line:10%
And it's treated with Firdapse — 3,4-DAP —

28
00:01:34.864 --> 00:01:40.293 line:10%
which blocks potassium channels to drive more calcium in, and more acetylcholine out.
